-- title:
--   Extensionality of ℚ-algebra maps on ℚ(j(qᵈ):d∣ N)
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $N$ (no nonvanishing hypothesis on $N$ is imposed) and a division ring $A$ carrying a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure. Let $f,g$ be $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra homomorphisms from [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305) to $A$, where `modularFunctionFieldFull N` is the intermediate field of the field of rational Laurent series `LaurentSeries ℚ` over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set `divisorExpansions N`, namely the set of those Laurent series of the form `qExpand ℚ d jq` for some $d$ with $d \neq 0$ and $d \mid N$; here `jq` is the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the power series `jNumQ` obtained from the integral series `jNum` by coefficientwise mapping to $\mathbb{Q}$, and `qExpand ℚ d` is the ring endomorphism of `LaurentSeries ℚ` that multiplies all exponents by $d$, i.e. substitutes $q \mapsto q^{d}$. The hypothesis is that for every natural number $d$ with $d \neq 0$ and every proof that $d \mid N$, the maps $f$ and $g$ take the same value at the element of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` given by `qExpand ℚ d jq` together with its membership witness `jqd_mem_full N hd`. The conclusion is that $f = g$ as $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra homomorphisms.
--
--   This is the extensionality principle for the function field of the modular curve generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$: a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra map out of it is determined by its values on those generators. It is used to identify maps and automorphisms of this field by their effect on the generators, for instance in recognising the Fricke-type automorphism characterised by $j(q^{a}) \mapsto j(q^{b})$ for $ab = N$, and in the verification of polynomial relations satisfied by the generators.
